    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "CD47 is a cell surface receptor over expressed on cancer cells that acts as a “don’t eat me” signal and inhibits the engulfment of cancer cells by phagocytes. This study explores the role of a novel monoclonal antibody, Hu5F9-G4, that blocks CD47’s interaction with its ligand SIRPα, in the phagocytosis of pancreatic cancer cells. Specifically, the study examined the therapeutic efficacy of the anti-CD47 (Hu5F9-G4) antibody alone and in combination with two EGFR targeting antibodies, cetuximab and panitumumab, in patient-derived pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC) xenografts. We found that the anti-CD47 antibody slowed tumor growth, but did not increase survival noticeably. When used in combination with Panitumumab and cetuximab, the anti-CD47 antibody lead to less tumor growth in vivo than treatment with panitumumab, cetuximab, or PBS alone did. While the combination treatment significantly reduced tumors, it only marginally increased survival over single agent anti-EGFR antibodies. We conducted protein expression profiling of EGFR and CD47 expression by FACS and in vitro phagocytosis assays, to determine the in vitro response of the cancerous cells to the anti-CD47 antibody Hu5F9G4, the anti-EGFR antibodies cetuximab and panitumumab, and the combination of these antibodies. The examination of the expression levels of CD47 and EGFR in the BxPC3 cell line showed the expression of both cell surface markers. The phagocytosis assay showed that blockade by Hu5F9-G4 alone and in combination with other antibodies in vitro enables phagocytosis of the cancer cells.  The combination treatment did not yield greater phagocytosis than the individual anti-EGFR treatments. This study is important because it is the first to demonstrate in the solid tumor setting to demonstrate that an anti-CD47 antibody in combination with an anti-EGFR antibody is able to dramatically shrink tumors in animal models.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
